--TEST--
`Core\Response::sendFile` answers with the file at its path rather than with a copy of it the program holds, and the bytes reach the body verbatim — `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's file row is the one body member that hands over a *name*. Inside a
// request it declares the file and the server streams it under the static-file
// policy's media-type table, which is where the range and the conditional
// already live, so nothing is ever read whole into memory. Off a request there
// is no response to declare onto, and then the bytes land in this program's own
// output instead -- `nvs_stdlib::response`'s gap 1 -- which is what makes them
// assertable from a case at all. The media type the policy gives them is
// asserted where the policy is, in `crates/nvs-server`.
Core\Response::sendFile("page.html");

// Verbatim, byte for byte: sending a file escapes nothing, substitutes nothing
// and re-frames nothing, which is the whole difference between this member and
// echoing what `Core\IO::read` answered.
Core\Response::sendFile("raw.txt");

// One body member called twice is one writer writing its whole body and not two
// (`rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`), so the second send
// follows the first in the order the program made them.
Core\Response::sendFile("page.html");
--FILE page.html--
<p>a page &amp; nothing more</p>
--FILE raw.txt--
{"held": "<b>&</b>"}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
<p>a page &amp; nothing more</p>
{"held": "<b>&</b>"}
<p>a page &amp; nothing more</p>
